### 4. Introduction to the Hackathon
* **Presentation**: Stuart Cheshire presented [New Participant Program - Session 4 - Introduction to the Hackathon](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-newparticipant-sessa-new-participant-program-session-4-introduction-to-the-hackathon-00).
* **Purpose & Core Philosophy**:
  * Organizers recognized: Charles Eckel, Benno Overeinder, and Barry Leiba.
  * The Hackathon embodies "running code" developed alongside draft specifications to uncover implementation flaws before final standardization (contrasted with PDF-only SDO processes). Industry bodies such as the Connectivity Standards Alliance (Matter protocol) have adopted this IETF model.
  * Held the weekend (Saturday/Sunday) preceding the IETF meeting week, ending with a Monday night demo social.
* **Logistics & Participation**:
  * Open to all for free without requiring IETF meeting registration; attracts local university students, open-source developers, and industry experts.
  * Projects are community-driven; anyone can list a project and nominate themselves as champion on the open Hackathon wiki.
* **Guidance for Presenting New Work & Receiving Feedback**:
  * Do not expect rubber-stamping or immediate praise for `draft-00` proposals.
  * Approach working groups with a clear problem statement and a call for help rather than asserting an unchangeable solution.
  * Treat microphone feedback gracefully (e.g., "Thank you for the suggestion, let's talk after") to leverage free technical input from domain experts.

